---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'My WiFi is slow but the wired connection is fast. What\'s wrong?',
    a: 'Your line is fine — the problem is between the router and your device. Move closer to the router, switch to the 5 GHz network if you\'re near it, move the router out of a cabinet and up high, and restart it. If the router is old, its Wi-Fi radio may simply be too weak for your package speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Both WiFi and wired are slow. Is it my line?',
    a: 'Probably. Test again early in the morning: if it\'s still slow on a cable at 7 am, the problem is your line or the network, not your home setup. Check the router lights, check for a known outage, then call your provider with your wired test results as evidence.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do the lights on my PTCL router mean?',
    a: 'On a fibre (Flash Fiber) ONT, the PON light should be solid green — a red or blinking LOS light means the fibre signal isn\'t reaching your home, and only PTCL can fix that. On a DSL modem, a red or blinking Internet/DSL light usually means a line or login problem. A restart fixes a stuck modem; it does not fix a red LOS light.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I check my DSL line quality?',
    a: 'Log in to your modem at 192.168.10.1 (or 192.168.1.1 on older modems) and open Status, then DSL or Device Info. Look at the downstream SNR margin — above 12 dB is good, below 6 dB causes drops and slow speed — and line attenuation — below 30 dB is good, above 50 dB means a long or damaged line. Quote both numbers when you complain to PTCL.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Slow Internet? Troubleshoot by Symptom', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/troubleshoot-slow-internet' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Slow Internet? Troubleshoot by Symptom — PTCL & Pakistan"
  description="WiFi slow but wired fast? Everything slow? Internet dropping? Find your symptom and work through the fix in order, with DSL line stats and when to call PTCL."
  canonical="/guides/troubleshoot-slow-internet"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Troubleshoot Slow Internet</p>
      <h1>Slow Internet? Troubleshoot by Symptom</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Don't guess — match what you're seeing to the symptom below and work through the
        fix in order. Start with a <a href="/speed-test">speed test</a> so you know your
        actual numbers before you change anything.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>Symptom: WiFi is slow, but wired is fast</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> your line is fine. The problem is between the router
        and your device.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Move closer to the router and test again — distance and walls are the usual cause.</li>
        <li>Connect to the <strong>5 GHz</strong> network if you're in the same room (fast, short range); use <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> further away (slower, longer range). Real-world 2.4 GHz rarely passes 35–45 Mbps, whatever your package.</li>
        <li>Move the router out of a cabinet, off the floor and up high, away from the TV and microwave.</li>
        <li>Restart the router — switch it off for 30 seconds, then on.</li>
        <li>If the router is several years old, its Wi-Fi radio may be too weak for your package speed — a newer router or a Wi-Fi extender may be the real fix.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Symptom: both WiFi and wired are slow</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> the problem is your line or the wider network, not
        your home setup.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Test again early in the morning. Fast at 7 am and slow at 9 pm means peak-hour congestion.</li>
        <li>Check the router lights (see below) — a red LOS or DSL light means a line fault.</li>
        <li>Check for a known outage or cable fault — see <a href="/guides/why-internet-slow-pakistan">why the internet is slow in Pakistan</a>.</li>
        <li>On DSL, check your line stats in the modem (below) and quote them to your provider.</li>
        <li>Call your provider with dated, wired test results. For PTCL, the helpline is 1218.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Symptom: the internet drops frequently</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> usually a physical line or hardware problem.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Check every cable: the phone line into the modem, the splitter, and the fibre cable into the ONT. A bent or crushed fibre cable kills the signal.</li>
        <li>On DSL, remove the splitter and plug the modem directly into the wall socket to test — a faulty splitter is a classic cause of drops.</li>
        <li>Check the modem isn't overheating — dusty, enclosed modems reboot themselves.</li>
        <li>On fibre, a blinking or red LOS light means the fibre signal is lost. Restarting won't fix it; report a fault.</li>
        <li>Drops that get worse when it rains usually mean a wet or damaged joint in the copper line — only PTCL can fix that.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Symptom: speed is fine, but video calls freeze</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> your download is fine, but upload or jitter is the
        problem — calls need steady two-way traffic, not just fast downloads.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Check your <strong>upload</strong> and <strong>jitter</strong> on the test, not just download. Upload under 2 Mbps or jitter over 30 ms will freeze calls.</li>
        <li>Pause cloud backups, photo sync and downloads during calls — uploads are the first thing they steal.</li>
        <li>Use 5 GHz Wi-Fi or a cable for the calling device.</li>
        <li>On DSL, upload is the weak link by design — if calls matter, a fibre upgrade is the real fix.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Symptom: slow only at night</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> peak-hour congestion. Evenings (roughly 7–11 pm) are
        the busiest hours on every Pakistani network. Test in the morning and again at
        9 pm and keep both results: if mornings are fast and evenings are slow, no router
        setting will fix it — but both results together are strong evidence for a
        complaint, and they tell you the line itself is healthy.
      </p>

      <h2>Symptom: one device is slow, the others are fine</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> the device, not the connection. Check for background
        updates or cloud sync running on that device, turn off any VPN, forget and
        rejoin the Wi-Fi network, and restart the device. On older laptops, a weak Wi-Fi
        adapter or an old driver can cap speed well below what the line delivers — test
        the same device over Ethernet to confirm.
      </p>

      <h2>Read your DSL line stats</h2>
      <p>
        If you're on PTCL DSL, your modem knows more about your line than any speed test.
        Log in at <code>192.168.10.1</code> (or <code>192.168.1.1</code> on older modems)
        and open <strong>Status &gt; DSL</strong> (or <strong>Device Info</strong>). Two
        numbers explain most slow DSL lines:
      </p>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Reading (downstream)</th><th>Good</th><th>Fair</th><th>Bad</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>SNR margin</strong></td><td>Above 12 dB</td><td>6–12 dB</td><td>Below 6 dB: drops and slow speed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Line attenuation</strong></td><td>Below 30 dB</td><td>30–50 dB</td><td>Above 50 dB: long or damaged line</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Quote both numbers when you complain — they turn "my internet is slow" into a
        specific line fault the technician can act on.
      </p>

      <h2>When to call your provider</h2>
      <p>
        Call when your <strong>wired</strong> speed stays below about 80% of your package
        at quiet times, when the LOS/DSL light is red, or when drops keep happening after
        you've checked cables and the splitter. Have ready: your account or landline
        number, dated test results, and (for DSL) your SNR and attenuation figures. For
        PTCL, the helpline is <strong>1218</strong> — ask for a complaint number and note
        it down.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Start with the numbers</h2>
        <p>Run a test before you change anything — then test again after each fix.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed">10 ways to improve speed →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
